<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niketas Choniates</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1155–<a href="/years/1217">1217</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Nicetas Choniates</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niketas Choniates was a Byzantine official and historian. His History covers the years 1118 to 1206 and is the main Greek source for the reigns of the Komnenian and Angelos emperors, the Bulgarian revolt of the Asen brothers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>. He witnessed the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, which he described vividly, and ended his life in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niketas-choniates.jpg" alt="Niketas Choniates" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Buchhändler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niketas_Choniates.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Niketas Choniates was born around 1155 in Chonae, near the present-day town of Honaz in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, into a family of lesser nobility. The family lacked the wealth of the Constantinopolitan aristocracy, but his father could afford to send Niketas and his brother Michael to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1164 for an education. Niketas studied rhetoric, literature, theology, law and politics under John Mauropous. He admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, whom he often blended with Christian theology.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He began as a junior tax official in the Province of Pontos around 1180, probably through connections made by his brother, and held several minor posts before becoming known as an orator. In 1189 Emperor Isaakios II made him Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippolis">Philippolis</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> marched through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a>, Isaakios first ordered him to rebuild the city's fortifications and then to tear down the new walls to keep the Germans from holding a fortified city. Niketas complied and fled just before the crusaders arrived. He later portrayed Isaakios as incompetent, passive and over-taxing.</p><p>In 1191 Isaakios made him Judge of the Velum, head of a tribunal for civil suits. In 1194, under Alexios III, he became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logothete">logothete</a> tou genikou, the highest post in the civil service and head of the senate. Alexios V dismissed him in early 1204 without a reason that Niketas accepted, and he stayed in the senate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>' fires in August 1203 destroyed Niketas's main residence, and he moved to a smaller house near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia</a>. During the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in April 1204 he hid with Venetian friends. On 17 April, once the plundering had eased, he left through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-gate-vladimir">Golden Gate</a> with his pregnant wife and the Patriarch John X Camaterus, carrying his infant son. He settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> at the court of Theodore I Lascaris, devoted himself to writing and died in 1217.</p><p>His Chronike diegesis, often published in English as O City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, is an eyewitness account of the sack. He also wrote the Thesaurus Orthodoxae Fidei, preserved complete in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> but published only in part, a chief source for the heresies of the twelfth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieronymus-wolf">Hieronymus Wolf</a> printed the History in 1557, Immanuel Bekker edited it in 1835, and Harry J. Magoulias translated it into English in 1984.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niketas Choniates” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Discontent grew under the Angelos emperors, and in 1185 an extraordinary tax levied by Isaac II Angelos, linked by the historian <b>Niketas Choniates</b> to the costs of the emperor's wedding to a Hungarian princess, provoked a revolt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Known mainly from the Byzantine historian <b>Niketas Choniates</b>, his realm rested on the near-impregnable fortress of Prosek and on several hundred warriors.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-prosek">Principality of Prosek</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chonae, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.76,29.27&amp;year=1195" aria-label="Chonae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5280 806 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Niketas Choniates"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5730 1076h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5730 1076h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.76,29.27&amp;year=1195">What was here in 1195? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
